Implementation of a New Operator Using new_other.sh
###################################################

new_unary.sh
************
If you are creating a new unary operator, it may be easier to use
tools/new_unary.sh.

NEW, OTHER
**********
We use NEW for the new operator's enum value
and OTHER for an already implemented similar operator's enum value.

Tools
*****
The instructions below assume that you have sourced the tools/tools_path.sh
file so that the tools directory is in your path.
For example, check_all.sh and git_commit.sh (referenced below) are in
the tools directory.

check_all.sh
============
If you run ``check_all.sh --no_plugin`` (instead of check_all.sh)
you will skip the testing of :ref:`src_gen <adfn_src_gen-name>` output
which takes more time than the other tests.

Sorting
*******
Let *nb* and *ne* be two positive integers.
If BEGIN\ _SORT_THIS_LINE_PLUS_\ *nb* appears in a source file,
the check_sort.sh tool (which is run during check_all.sh)
will automatically sort source code lines beginning
*nb* lines after the text.  This sorting will end *ne* lines before text
END\ _SORT_THIS_LINE_MINUS_\ *ne* .

NEW_op.cpp TensorType
*********************
The file NEW_op.cpp will specify how forward_par, forward_var, forward_der
and reverse_der functions are calculated for
TensorType equal to at:Tensor and adten_t.
Sometimes forward_par and forward_var are not yet available for
one or more operations that are used to compute reverse_der.
You can set the corresponding function as not yet implemented for adten_t
arguments by changing
{xrst_code cpp}
    template void NEW_op_t<adten_t>::reverse_der(
        size_t                       op_index    ,
        const agraph_t&              agraph      ,
        const vector<at::Tensor>&    con_vec     ,
        const vector<adten_t>&       par_all     ,
        const vector<adten_t>&       var_all     ,
        vector<adten_t>&             rev_der
    ) const;
{xrst_code}
to
{xrst_code cpp}
    // TODO: use template version of reverse_der to implement this once
    // forward_var and forward_par are implemented for the
    // list_of_needed_adten_operations.
    template <> void NEW_op_t<adten_t>::reverse_der(
        size_t                       op_index    ,
        const agraph_t&              agraph      ,
        const vector<at::Tensor>&    con_vec     ,
        const vector<adten_t>&       par_all     ,
        const vector<adten_t>&       var_all     ,
        vector<adten_t>&             rev_der
    ) const {
        user_assert(false,
        "reverse_der not yet implemented for NEW with adten_t arguments" );
    }
{xrst_code}
where list_of_needed_adten_operations is replaced by the needed operators.
Some operators may require a totally separate implementation for at::Tensor
and adten_t arguments. This requires more source code and more testing
and will be harder to maintain.

Step 1: Create NEW operator
***************************

Branch
======
Create a new git branch called NEW and the run
{xrst_code sh}
    new_other.sh name_new name_other
{xrst_code}
where *name_new* is the name of the operator you are implementing
and *name_other* is the name of a similar already implemented operator.

Review changes to the following files (should not need editing)
===============================================================
#.  examples/CMakeLists.txt
#.  include/ad_tensor/dev/derive_op.hpp
#.  src/CMakeLists.txt
#.  src/dev/enum2derive.cpp
#.  src/dev/to_string.cpp

Make minor corrections changes in the following files
=====================================================
#.  include/ad_tensor/adten.hpp:
    The API interface for the new function is either
    adten_t::NEW or ad_tensor::NEW.
    You should place your new prototype (possibly more than one)
    below the comment about functions being in alphabetical order.
    Prototypes in this file are not automatically sorted because some
    are more than one line long.

#.  include/ad_tensor/dev/op_enum.hpp:
    Choose a short description as a one line comment just after the
    operator enum value.

The following file will require a lot of corrections
====================================================
#.  src/adten/NAME.cpp


Test and Commit changes
=======================
Run check_all.sh to check that the changes above compile and that its
documentation builds.
Once the errors are fixed, commit the changes using git_commit.sh.

Step 2: Implement AD Function for Function Values
*************************************************

src/dev/derive_op/NEW_op.cpp
============================
Implement the forward_par and forward_var for the NEW operator
(use src/dev/derive_op/OTHER_op.cpp as an example for how to do this).
The function forward_par determines the parameter with index op_index.
During this functions, the parameters with index less that op_index
are known and none of the variables are known.
The function forward_var determines the variable with index op_index.
During this function, all the parameters are known
and the variables with index less that op_index are known.

examples/adten/NEW.cpp
======================
Change this example (created in Step 1) to test an AD function
that uses the NEW operator's function values.

Test and Commit NEW.cpp Example
===============================
Run check_all.sh to check that the modified example compiles, runs,
and passes it tests.

Step 3: Implement Forward Derivative Evaluation
***********************************************

src/dev/derive_op/NEW_op.cpp
============================
Implement the forward_der for the NEW operator
(use src/dev/derive_op/OTHER_op.cpp as an example for how to do this).
The function forward_der determines the forward derivative with index op_index.
During this function, all the parameters and variables are known
and the forward derivatives with index less that op_index are known.

examples/adten/NEW.cpp
======================
Add a test of forward_der for the NEW operator;
see examples/adten/OTHER.cpp or tests/adten/OTHER.cpp.

Test and Commit NEW.cpp Test
============================
Run check_all.sh to check that the modified example compiles, runs,
and passes it tests.

Step 4: Implement Reverse Derivative Evaluation
***********************************************

src/dev/derive_op/NEW_op.cpp
============================
Implement the reverse_der for the NEW operator
(use src/dev/derive_op/OTHER_op.cpp as an example for how to do this).
On input, the partial of the objective w.r.t. each of the variables
with indices less than or equal op_index are known.
Upon return, the result of the operator with index op_index
is removed (as an argument to the objective)
and its contribution is added to each of the NEW operator arguments.

examples/adten/NEW.cpp
======================
Add a test of reverse_der for the NEW operator;
see examples/adten/OTHER.cpp or tests/adten/OTHER.cpp.

Test and Commit NEW.cpp Test
============================
Run check_all.sh to check that the modified example compiles, runs,
and passes it tests.

Step 5: Implement Source Code Generation
****************************************

src/dev/derive_op/NEW_op.cpp
============================
Implement the src_gen for the NEW operator,
use src/dev/derive_op/OTHER_op.cpp as an example for how to do this.
Note that only the at::Tensor version of src_gen should be implemented
(the adten_t version is not used).
Also note that src_gen evaluates function values similar to forward_par
and forward_var for the NEW operator.

tests/adfn/src_gen.cpp
======================
Add a test of the NEW operator to this test.
All the operators use this src_gen test.
This reduces the number of times the compiler is launched during testing
(because it takes a significant amount of time).

Test and Commit src_gen.cpp
===========================
Run ``check_all.sh`` (not ``check_all.sh --no_plugin``)
to check that the modified example compiles, runs, and passes it tests.


{xrst_end new_op}
